  "summary": "The house-music wave and its visual culture: smiley faces, day-glo colors, baggy dress and warehouse ecstasy.",
  "description": "Chicago acid house (Phuture's \"Acid Tracks\", 1987) crossed to Britain where 1988's Second Summer of Love turned it into a mass youth movement: smiley logos, polka-dot fluorescence, oversized \"smiley\" tees, whistle culture and illegal warehouse parties. Its graphics — Damion? — and club flyers (Boy's Own) rewired British design; it spawned rave, hardcore and the whole EDM lineage. Criminalized and commercialized by 1990-92, its optimism is iconic.",
